.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
runs programs written in Wattle,
a Lisp-like programming language.
Wattle reimplements the virtual machine,
compiler and core library from the Janet programming language
in Zig with a syntax inspired by Clojure.
.Pp
.Nm
has four subcommands.
.Ic run
runs a script,
evaluates code or starts a REPL (read-eval-print loop).
.Ic check
compiles a script without running it.
.Ic build
makes an image from source.
.Ic twig
manages installed packages.
.Ic help
describes a subcommand.
.Pp
.Ic run
is the subcommand when no other is named.
Where
.Nm
would otherwise report an unrecognised subcommand or option,
or no subcommand,
it reads the rest of the command line as the arguments of
.Ic run .
The command lines
.Ql "wattle" ,
.Ql "wattle hello\&.wattle"
and
.Ql "wattle -e \(aq\*(lp+ 1 2\*(rp\(aq"
are therefore the same as
.Ql "wattle run" ,
.Ql "wattle run hello\&.wattle"
and
.Ql "wattle run -e \(aq\*(lp+ 1 2\*(rp\(aq" .
A mistyped subcommand is read as the name of a script,
and a script named after a subcommand is run with
.Ql "wattle run"
in front of it.
.Pp
.Fl h ,
.Fl v
and
.Fl m
are read only before the subcommand name.
A subcommand does not read them again.
.Pp
An option that takes a value can be written
.Ql "--eval=code" ,
and short options can be combined,
as in
.Ql "-qr" .
.Ql "--"
stops the processing of options.
.Pp
An unrecognised option,
an option missing its value or a missing parameter prints a
message and a pointer to
.Fl "-help"
on standard error and exits with status 1.
.Pp
.Nm
exits when the script and any tasks it scheduled on the event
loop have finished.
.
.Ss Options
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Xo
.Fl h ,
.Fl "-help"
.Xc
Print a usage summary and exit.
Each subcommand also reads
.Fl h ,
and prints its own usage.
.It Xo
.Fl v ,
.Fl "-version"
.Xc
Print the version of Wattle and exit.
.It Xo
.Fl m ,
.Fl "-syspath"
.Ar path
.Xc
